========================================================================================
 Denied request trace: chronological denial log with candidate policies, in LogScale
========================================================================================
 The question this answers: a token got "permission denied." Which of its policies did
 it, and what else was this identity doing around the same time?

 STATUS: UNVERIFIED. Written against the verified-live-audit-device field reference in
 grafana/README.md and the splunk/ field notes, not run against a real LogScale
 instance. Nobody on our side has a LogScale instance to test against.
 Treat this as the logic in concrete form, not as tested code. Confirm syntax and field
 names against one real denied-request audit entry before relying on any result.

 WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, BY DESIGN OF THE AUDIT LOG ITSELF (not a query limitation):
 - It narrows to a CANDIDATE list of policies, it does not name the one that denied the
   request. A Vault ACL "permission denied" error lists every policy attached to the
   token (auth.policies), not which one lacked the capability. Verified against a real
   throwaway Vault audit log, 2026-10-01: a token with policies ["default","narrow"]
   denied a read produced error="permission denied" and auth.policies=["default",
   "narrow"] -- both listed, neither singled out. With one non-default policy the
   candidate list IS the answer; with several it is not.
 - It cannot tell you whether a Sentinel RGP/EGP fired instead of an ACL check, or what
   that looks like in `error`. UNCONFIRMED -- ask on a live Enterprise cluster. If the
   error string differs by denial type (ACL vs RGP vs EGP), capture a real example and
   update this file and the companion scenario doc before shipping either as fact.
 - CONFIRMED 2026-10-01 (throwaway OSS Vault, root namespace): auth.policies IS the full
   merged candidate set, group-derived policies included -- it is not limited to
   auth.token_policies. A group-granted policy showed up in auth.policies AND separately
   in auth.identity_policies on every request, success or denial, for an entity-linked
   token. This query pulls auth.policies specifically because it is already the superset;
   add auth.identity_policies to the table/groupBy below if you want the group-derived
   ones called out on their own.
 - STILL UNCONFIRMED: the above was root namespace only, no Enterprise license available
   to test. Whether a CROSS-namespace grant (external_namespace_policies: an external
   group in one namespace granting a policy via membership in an internal group inside a
   different namespace) shows up in auth.policies the same way is unverified. Check this
   on a real namespaced cluster before trusting a "no cross-namespace policy" read from
   this query alone -- see the companion scenario doc, Part 2b.
 - request.remote_address may not be the real client IP if your load balancer does not
   preserve it (see grafana/README.md). Don't build attribution on it without confirming.

 Nested JSON fields come through the audit event body; adjust the parse stage below to
 however your LogScale repo is configured (#type/structuredData vs raw JSON parse).
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. Chronological denial trace for one path or one identity
     The "search through X Y Z path and get the trace, chronological" view: walk every
     denial against one path, or everything one identity hit, in time order. Swap the
     filter line for either a path or an identity; leave the other broad to see
     everything nearby.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
// pick ONE of the next two lines, comment out the other
| request.path = "secret/data/prod/db-creds"
// | auth.display_name = "oidc-jsmith"
| sort(@timestamp, order=asc)
| table([@timestamp, request.namespace.id, request.operation, request.path,
         auth.display_name, auth.entity_id, auth.policies, auth.identity_policies, error])


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. Candidate-policy summary: which policies show up on denials, by path
     Rolls the trace above into one row per (namespace, path, identity): every policy
     that was on the token at denial time, and how many denials. Still a candidate set,
     not an attribution -- see status note above.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
| groupBy([request.namespace.id, request.path, auth.display_name, auth.entity_id],
          function=[count(as=denials),
                    collect(auth.policies, as=candidate_policies),
                    collect(error, as=error_samples),
                    max(@timestamp, as=last_denied)])
| sort(denials, order=desc)


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. Sanity check: does this repo have denial events at all, and do they carry auth.policies?
     RUN THIS FIRST against a real environment. If candidate_policy_coverage is 0, the
     audit device or the LogScale parser is dropping the auth block and nothing below
     this line will ever return a usable candidate list.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
| test(auth.policies != null) | count(as=with_policies)
